/--
The open core of the conjecture: 64 is not a periodic point of the Collatz
permutation A006368.  Since the map is a bijection of ℕ, this is equivalent to
the unboundedness of the trajectory of 64, i.e. to an instance of the
"original Collatz problem" (L. Collatz, 1932; cf. SIAM Review Problem 63-13,
the discussions by Shanks (1965) and Atkin (1966), and Guy, *Unsolved Problems
in Number Theory*, E17), which has been open for over 90 years: it is not even
known whether *any* trajectory of this permutation is infinite.

Status notes (results of an extensive settlement attempt):

* The map has the elegant equivalent form `f(2n) = 3n`, `f(4n±1) = 3n±1`; it is
  a bijection of ℕ whose inverse is A006369 (`g(3n) = 2n`, `g(3n±1) = 4n±1`).
* A collision `a i = a j` with `i < j` would force `f^[j-i] 64 = 64` (see the
  main proof below), i.e. a cycle through 64; conversely a cycle refutes the
  conjecture, so the conjecture is *exactly* the statement that 64 is not
  periodic, equivalently (by bijectivity) that its orbit is unbounded.
* Exact computation: the orbit makes no return within 3·10⁷ steps; at that
  point values have 2,546,259 bits, and the bit-length grows linearly at the
  ergodic-drift rate ½·log₂(9/8) ≈ 0.085 bits/step; the orbit never dips below
  64 after the start.  Hence any cycle through 64 would have period > 3·10⁷
  and would contain integers exceeding 2^2546258.  Its itinerary statistics match the Bernoulli(½,¼,¼)
  measure of the induced 3-to-1 expanding map on ℤ₂ (which has fixed points
  0, ±1 and is measure-theoretically a full 3-shift).
* No finite/congruence certificate can prove the conjecture: forward images of
  residue classes coarsen 2-adic precision (so any f-closed finite union of
  2-power classes is trivial), the three affine branches form a *complete*
  contracting iterated-function system mod 3^B (so reachability mod 3^B is
  full — verified: all 3⁷ classes are reached, and the actual orbit visits all
  of them), and the branch maps generate translations modulo every prime.
  Consistently with this, the hypothetical cycle is locally consistent at every
  finite precision, as witnessed by the backward orbit 85, 113, 151, 201, ….
* The cycle ("Diophantine") equation `64·(2^D − 3^p) = S(w)` determines the
  itinerary word `w` uniquely as the orbit's own itinerary, so all approaches
  through it are circular; Baker-type lower bounds do not apply because the
  required value of the linear form `p·log 3 − D·log 2` is ≈ −Σ log(1±1/(3xᵢ)),
  not near 0; and the bounded-remainder-word rigidity available for cycles with
  small max/min ratio is vacuous here (the window has width log₂(M/m) ≳ 10⁵).

Hence this lemma is a genuinely open problem; no proof with presently known
mathematics is available, and the `sorry` below marks precisely that gap.
-/
theorem A006368_no_cycle_through_64 :
    ∀ p : ℕ, 0 < p → A006368_map^[p] 64 ≠ 64 := by
  sorry

/--
It is conjectured that this trajectory does not close on itself.
This is equivalent to stating that the sequence is injective on positive indices.
-/
theorem oeis_223086_conjecture_0 :
  ∀ (i j : ℕ), 0 < i → 0 < j → a i = a j → i = j :=
  conjecture_iff_no_cycle.mpr A006368_no_cycle_through_64
